# Exceptions to the runtime dependency audit, one advisory per entry.
#
# `npm run check:audit`, the Dependency audit step in CI's Verify, fails on any HIGH or CRITICAL
# advisory that `npm audit --omit=dev` reports (docs/PROCESS.md, Upgrades, says what that covers)
# unless an entry here allows it. app/scripts/audit-allowlist.ts parses this file.
#
# An entry is the last resort, for an advisory that no fixed version can reach yet. First upgrade
# the package, then try an `overrides` entry in app/package.json with its reason in the
# `//overrides` note (docs/PROCESS.md, Upgrades). Check `npm view <package> versions` against the
# advisory's affected range before deciding that there's no fix.
#
# Each entry is the advisory's GHSA id and an expiry date, with comments beneath it saying why it's
# allowed and what would lift it:
#
#   GHSA-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx exp:YYYY-MM-DD
#   # Why it's allowed, and what lifts it.
#
# - An entry is an advisory, not a package. npm also flags each package that only depends on a
#   vulnerable one, and the entry covers those too. A new advisory against any of them still fails.
# - An entry stops working on its expiry date: exp:2026-12-01 allows its advisory through
#   2026-11-30. Then take the fix, or set a new date and say in the comment what changed, so that
#   an extension is a decision rather than a renewal.
# - Nothing deletes an entry when its advisory is fixed. The gate warns about each entry that
#   matches no HIGH or CRITICAL advisory, and then you delete it and its comment.
# - npm's numeric advisory id works in place of the GHSA id, but prefer the GHSA id.
#
# Add an entry only for a real finding, never ahead of one.

